1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,456
A tower crane is a thin steel

2
00:00:02,456 --> 00:00:06,015
pole, sometimes taller than a thirty story

3
00:00:06,015 --> 00:00:09,234
building, with a long arm sticking out

4
00:00:09,234 --> 00:00:10,251
to one side.

5
00:00:10,431 --> 00:00:12,730
It lifts tonnes of steel over the

6
00:00:12,730 --> 00:00:13,844
heads of people,

7
00:00:13,844 --> 00:00:15,307
and on a windy night,

8
00:00:15,307 --> 00:00:16,422
nobody is in it.

9
00:00:16,602 --> 00:00:18,197
By every instinct,

10
00:00:18,197 --> 00:00:19,969
it should fall over.

11
00:00:20,149 --> 00:00:21,995
It almost never does.

12
00:00:22,175 --> 00:00:25,675
Today, eleven parts of a tower crane,

13
00:00:25,675 --> 00:00:27,945
and why each one exists.

14
00:00:28,295 --> 00:00:32,033
Tower cranes build almost every tall building

15
00:00:32,033 --> 00:00:33,113
in the world.

16
00:00:33,293 --> 00:00:35,085
They are put up in pieces,

17
00:00:35,085 --> 00:00:37,014
they grow with the building,

18
00:00:37,014 --> 00:00:38,737
and when the job is done,

19
00:00:38,737 --> 00:00:39,771
they disappear.

20
00:00:39,951 --> 00:00:42,683
Most people walk past them every day

21
00:00:42,683 --> 00:00:45,264
and never ask how a structure that

22
00:00:45,264 --> 00:00:48,148
looks this fragile can lift this much.

23
00:00:48,498 --> 00:00:51,364
As always, every fact comes from a

24
00:00:51,364 --> 00:00:53,640
named source, crane makers,

25
00:00:53,640 --> 00:00:57,602
safety agencies, and official accident reports,

26
00:00:57,602 --> 00:01:00,131
all listed in the description.

27
00:01:00,311 --> 00:01:02,864
Let's start with the most important part,

28
00:01:02,864 --> 00:01:04,421
the one on the short arm.

29
00:01:04,771 --> 00:01:07,381
Why does a tower crane carry heavy

30
00:01:07,381 --> 00:01:09,914
concrete blocks on its short arm?

31
00:01:10,094 --> 00:01:12,694
Because it is a giant seesaw.

32
00:01:12,874 --> 00:01:13,889
The long arm,

33
00:01:13,889 --> 00:01:15,059
called the jib,

34
00:01:15,059 --> 00:01:17,478
carries the load on one side of

35
00:01:17,478 --> 00:01:18,180
the mast.

36
00:01:18,360 --> 00:01:19,430
The short arm,

37
00:01:19,430 --> 00:01:21,187
called the counter jib,

38
00:01:21,187 --> 00:01:24,548
carries the counterweight on the other side.

39
00:01:24,728 --> 00:01:27,330
The blocks balance the weight of the

40
00:01:27,330 --> 00:01:29,136
jib and part of the load,

41
00:01:29,136 --> 00:01:31,810
so the mast is pushed mostly straight

42
00:01:31,810 --> 00:01:33,761
down, not bent to one side.

43
00:01:34,111 --> 00:01:37,101
The counterweight can be made of several

44
00:01:37,101 --> 00:01:39,792
concrete slabs, adding up to tens of

45
00:01:39,792 --> 00:01:41,436
tonnes on a big crane.

46
00:01:41,616 --> 00:01:43,265
It is fixed in place,

47
00:01:43,265 --> 00:01:46,562
so it cannot balance every load perfectly.

48
00:01:46,742 --> 00:01:48,148
When the hook is empty,

49
00:01:48,148 --> 00:01:50,287
the crane leans a little toward the

50
00:01:50,287 --> 00:01:51,142
counterweight.

51
00:01:51,322 --> 00:01:53,235
When it lifts a heavy load,

52
00:01:53,235 --> 00:01:54,863
it leans the other way.

53
00:01:55,043 --> 00:01:57,888
The mast is designed to handle both.

54
00:01:58,238 --> 00:02:01,244
That is also why the counterweight blocks

55
00:02:01,244 --> 00:02:03,371
are put on in a precise order

56
00:02:03,371 --> 00:02:05,424
when the crane is assembled.

57
00:02:05,604 --> 00:02:07,956
If the jib is not installed yet

58
00:02:07,956 --> 00:02:10,232
and too many blocks are added,

59
00:02:10,232 --> 00:02:12,280
the crane can tip backward.

60
00:02:12,460 --> 00:02:15,074
Every step of putting a crane together

61
00:02:15,074 --> 00:02:17,963
follows a written sequence from the maker.

62
00:02:18,313 --> 00:02:20,519
Why can a tower crane lift a

63
00:02:20,519 --> 00:02:23,119
heavy load near the mast but only

64
00:02:23,119 --> 00:02:25,089
a small one at the tip of

65
00:02:25,089 --> 00:02:25,720
its jib?

66
00:02:25,900 --> 00:02:28,344
Because what matters is not just the

67
00:02:28,344 --> 00:02:30,652
weight, it is the weight times the

68
00:02:30,652 --> 00:02:31,263
distance.

69
00:02:31,443 --> 00:02:34,168
Ten tonnes close to the mast twists

70
00:02:34,168 --> 00:02:36,893
the crane much less than ten tonnes

71
00:02:36,893 --> 00:02:38,061
at the far end.

72
00:02:38,241 --> 00:02:40,712
So the farther out the trolley carries

73
00:02:40,712 --> 00:02:42,857
the hook, the lighter the load it

74
00:02:42,857 --> 00:02:44,093
is allowed to lift.

75
00:02:44,443 --> 00:02:46,862
Every crane has a load chart,

76
00:02:46,862 --> 00:02:49,781
a table that shows the maximum load

77
00:02:49,781 --> 00:02:51,200
at each distance.

78
00:02:51,380 --> 00:02:53,917
A crane that can lift around twelve

79
00:02:53,917 --> 00:02:56,454
tonnes near the mast may be allowed

80
00:02:56,454 --> 00:02:58,701
only two or three tonnes at the

81
00:02:58,701 --> 00:03:00,006
tip of a long jib.

82
00:03:00,186 --> 00:03:02,678
The operator is trained to read it,

83
00:03:02,678 --> 00:03:04,957
but the crane does not trust the

84
00:03:04,957 --> 00:03:06,026
operator alone.

85
00:03:06,376 --> 00:03:09,989
Inside, devices called limiters measure the load

86
00:03:09,989 --> 00:03:12,549
on the hoist rope and the position

87
00:03:12,549 --> 00:03:13,678
of the trolley.

88
00:03:13,858 --> 00:03:16,362
If a lift goes over the limit,

89
00:03:16,362 --> 00:03:17,864
the crane refuses.

90
00:03:18,044 --> 00:03:20,708
It stops the hoist from lifting higher

91
00:03:20,708 --> 00:03:23,373
and stops the trolley from moving out.

92
00:03:23,553 --> 00:03:25,455
It can only move in the safe

93
00:03:25,455 --> 00:03:28,310
direction, bringing the load closer to the

94
00:03:28,310 --> 00:03:28,649
mast.

95
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:31,053
Why does a tower crane need a

96
00:03:31,053 --> 00:03:33,320
huge block of concrete under it,

97
00:03:33,320 --> 00:03:34,665
hidden underground?

98
00:03:34,845 --> 00:03:38,027
Because the counterweight can only balance part

99
00:03:38,027 --> 00:03:38,839
of the load.

100
00:03:39,019 --> 00:03:41,236
The rest of the tipping force goes

101
00:03:41,236 --> 00:03:42,932
down the mast to the base.

102
00:03:43,112 --> 00:03:45,422
The bottom of the mast is bolted

103
00:03:45,422 --> 00:03:48,527
to anchors cast into a concrete foundation,

104
00:03:48,527 --> 00:03:50,765
often hundreds of tonnes of it,

105
00:03:50,765 --> 00:03:53,508
which holds the crane upright like the

106
00:03:53,508 --> 00:03:54,664
roots of a tree.

107
00:03:55,014 --> 00:03:57,861
The foundation is designed for the worst

108
00:03:57,861 --> 00:04:00,423
case, the strongest wind at the site

109
00:04:00,423 --> 00:04:02,630
plus the crane's heaviest load.

110
00:04:02,810 --> 00:04:05,724
Engineers check the soil under it too.

111
00:04:05,904 --> 00:04:08,640
Weak ground can let the foundation tilt,

112
00:04:08,640 --> 00:04:10,625
and a crane that leans even a

113
00:04:10,625 --> 00:04:12,814
little at the bottom leans a lot

114
00:04:12,814 --> 00:04:13,566
at the top.

115
00:04:13,917 --> 00:04:15,628
In two thousand six,

116
00:04:15,628 --> 00:04:17,681
in Bellevue, Washington,

117
00:04:17,681 --> 00:04:21,360
a tower crane fell onto apartment buildings

118
00:04:21,360 --> 00:04:22,643
during a storm,

119
00:04:22,643 --> 00:04:25,039
and a man inside was killed.

120
00:04:25,219 --> 00:04:28,238
Investigators found that the steel base the

121
00:04:28,238 --> 00:04:31,047
crane stood on had been poorly designed.

122
00:04:31,227 --> 00:04:34,973
Since then, many cities require engineers to

123
00:04:34,973 --> 00:04:38,464
approve every crane base before the crane

124
00:04:38,464 --> 00:04:39,145
goes up.

125
00:04:39,495 --> 00:04:41,947
How does a tower crane get taller

126
00:04:41,947 --> 00:04:43,581
as the building rises?

127
00:04:43,761 --> 00:04:45,247
It lifts itself.

128
00:04:45,427 --> 00:04:48,105
A climbing frame around the mast has

129
00:04:48,105 --> 00:04:51,005
hydraulic jacks that push the whole top

130
00:04:51,005 --> 00:04:52,790
of the crane up by a few

131
00:04:52,790 --> 00:04:53,311
meters.

132
00:04:53,491 --> 00:04:54,725
That opens a gap,

133
00:04:54,725 --> 00:04:56,977
and the crane uses its own hook

134
00:04:56,977 --> 00:04:59,229
to lift a new mast section into

135
00:04:59,229 --> 00:04:59,446
it.

136
00:04:59,626 --> 00:05:01,667
The section is bolted in,

137
00:05:01,667 --> 00:05:04,280
and the crane has grown one step

138
00:05:04,280 --> 00:05:04,851
taller.

139
00:05:05,201 --> 00:05:07,705
A crane standing on its own can

140
00:05:07,705 --> 00:05:10,210
only go so high before the wind

141
00:05:10,210 --> 00:05:12,715
and the loads bend the mast too

142
00:05:12,715 --> 00:05:13,119
much.

143
00:05:13,299 --> 00:05:15,683
So tall cranes are tied to the

144
00:05:15,683 --> 00:05:19,181
building with steel collars and braces every

145
00:05:19,181 --> 00:05:20,056
few floors.

146
00:05:20,236 --> 00:05:22,968
The building that the crane is building

147
00:05:22,968 --> 00:05:24,369
becomes its support.

148
00:05:24,719 --> 00:05:27,235
Climbing is one of the most dangerous

149
00:05:27,235 --> 00:05:29,003
moments in a crane's life.

150
00:05:29,183 --> 00:05:30,548
During the jump,

151
00:05:30,548 --> 00:05:32,938
the top of the crane is held

152
00:05:32,938 --> 00:05:35,243
only by the climbing frame.

153
00:05:35,423 --> 00:05:37,164
Wind limits are lower,

154
00:05:37,164 --> 00:05:39,776
the jib must be balanced exactly,

155
00:05:39,776 --> 00:05:42,783
and nobody is allowed to lift anything

156
00:05:42,783 --> 00:05:43,179
else.

157
00:05:43,359 --> 00:05:44,819
It is done slowly,

158
00:05:44,819 --> 00:05:46,441
by specialist crews,

159
00:05:46,441 --> 00:05:50,092
following the maker's procedure step by step.

160
00:05:50,443 --> 00:05:53,560
Why do crane operators release the brakes

161
00:05:53,560 --> 00:05:55,917
at night, so the crane can spin

162
00:05:55,917 --> 00:05:57,362
freely in the wind?

163
00:05:57,542 --> 00:06:00,174
Because a crane fighting the wind is

164
00:06:00,174 --> 00:06:02,732
in danger, and a crane turning with

165
00:06:02,732 --> 00:06:03,463
it is not.

166
00:06:03,643 --> 00:06:06,189
When the slewing brake is released,

167
00:06:06,189 --> 00:06:08,808
the wind pushes the jib around until

168
00:06:08,808 --> 00:06:10,191
it points downwind,

169
00:06:10,191 --> 00:06:11,573
like a weathervane.

170
00:06:11,753 --> 00:06:14,288
The long jib then shows its narrow

171
00:06:14,288 --> 00:06:15,481
end to the wind,

172
00:06:15,481 --> 00:06:17,941
and the force on the crane drops.

173
00:06:18,291 --> 00:06:19,998
If the brake were locked,

174
00:06:19,998 --> 00:06:22,115
a storm hitting the side of the

175
00:06:22,115 --> 00:06:24,505
jib could push with enormous force,

176
00:06:24,505 --> 00:06:26,963
and the crane might break or topple.

177
00:06:27,143 --> 00:06:29,456
So the rule on most sites is

178
00:06:29,456 --> 00:06:30,034
simple.

179
00:06:30,214 --> 00:06:31,444
When work stops,

180
00:06:31,444 --> 00:06:32,597
raise the hook,

181
00:06:32,597 --> 00:06:33,903
park the trolley,

182
00:06:33,903 --> 00:06:36,286
and set the crane to free slew.

183
00:06:36,466 --> 00:06:39,071
People who see cranes turning on their

184
00:06:39,071 --> 00:06:41,402
own at night are watching a safety

185
00:06:41,402 --> 00:06:42,225
system work.

186
00:06:42,575 --> 00:06:43,784
During the day,

187
00:06:43,784 --> 00:06:46,766
cranes have an anemometer on top that

188
00:06:46,766 --> 00:06:48,217
measures the wind.

189
00:06:48,397 --> 00:06:50,152
Above a certain speed,

190
00:06:50,152 --> 00:06:53,582
often around seventy kilometers an hour for

191
00:06:53,582 --> 00:06:56,294
normal work and lower for big flat

192
00:06:56,294 --> 00:06:58,289
loads, lifting must stop.

193
00:06:58,469 --> 00:07:00,749
A sheet of plywood or a glass

194
00:07:00,749 --> 00:07:02,557
panel acts like a sail,

195
00:07:02,557 --> 00:07:04,837
and the wind can swing it out

196
00:07:04,837 --> 00:07:05,702
of control.

197
00:07:06,052 --> 00:07:08,104
Why does the top of a tower

198
00:07:08,104 --> 00:07:10,080
crane sit on a giant ring?

199
00:07:10,260 --> 00:07:12,538
Because the whole upper crane,

200
00:07:12,538 --> 00:07:14,437
the jib, the counter jib,

201
00:07:14,437 --> 00:07:16,715
the counterweight and the cab,

202
00:07:16,715 --> 00:07:18,842
has to turn around on top of

203
00:07:18,842 --> 00:07:21,348
the mast while carrying the load.

204
00:07:21,528 --> 00:07:23,850
The slewing ring is a huge ball

205
00:07:23,850 --> 00:07:26,472
or roller bearing that holds all of

206
00:07:26,472 --> 00:07:29,844
that weight, while letting it rotate smoothly

207
00:07:29,844 --> 00:07:31,118
in a full circle.

208
00:07:31,468 --> 00:07:34,004
The ring has gear teeth cut into

209
00:07:34,004 --> 00:07:34,242
it.

210
00:07:34,422 --> 00:07:38,303
Electric motors with small gears turn against

211
00:07:38,303 --> 00:07:41,667
those teeth, slowly rotating the crane.

212
00:07:41,847 --> 00:07:45,118
Frequency drives let them start and stop

213
00:07:45,118 --> 00:07:48,552
very smoothly, because a sudden stop would

214
00:07:48,552 --> 00:07:51,496
make the load swing like a pendulum,

215
00:07:51,496 --> 00:07:53,213
tens of meters below.

216
00:07:53,563 --> 00:07:55,966
The bolts that hold the slewing ring

217
00:07:55,966 --> 00:07:58,236
are among the most critical in the

218
00:07:58,236 --> 00:07:58,636
crane.

219
00:07:58,816 --> 00:08:01,352
They are tightened to an exact force

220
00:08:01,352 --> 00:08:03,042
and inspected regularly.

221
00:08:03,222 --> 00:08:05,184
If they loosen or crack,

222
00:08:05,184 --> 00:08:07,882
the top of the crane can separate

223
00:08:07,882 --> 00:08:09,027
from the mast.

224
00:08:09,207 --> 00:08:11,681
A few bolts are all that hold

225
00:08:11,681 --> 00:08:13,131
the top in place.

226
00:08:13,482 --> 00:08:16,366
Why does the crane operator sit in

227
00:08:16,366 --> 00:08:18,317
a small cab at the top,

228
00:08:18,317 --> 00:08:19,844
tens of meters up?

229
00:08:20,024 --> 00:08:21,417
Because from there,

230
00:08:21,417 --> 00:08:23,543
they can see most of the site

231
00:08:23,543 --> 00:08:25,376
and the path of the load.

232
00:08:25,556 --> 00:08:26,942
On a busy site,

233
00:08:26,942 --> 00:08:29,713
the hook travels over workers,

234
00:08:29,713 --> 00:08:31,837
roads and other cranes.

235
00:08:32,017 --> 00:08:33,863
Being high up and close to the

236
00:08:33,863 --> 00:08:36,324
slewing ring also lets the operator feel

237
00:08:36,324 --> 00:08:37,555
how the crane moves.

238
00:08:37,905 --> 00:08:41,542
Getting there means climbing ladders inside the

239
00:08:41,542 --> 00:08:44,716
mast, sometimes for more than ten minutes

240
00:08:44,716 --> 00:08:46,264
on very tall cranes.

241
00:08:46,444 --> 00:08:48,768
So many operators go up once at

242
00:08:48,768 --> 00:08:51,093
the start of the shift and stay

243
00:08:51,093 --> 00:08:52,142
there all day,

244
00:08:52,142 --> 00:08:53,642
with food and water.

245
00:08:53,822 --> 00:08:56,075
Some newer cranes have lifts in the

246
00:08:56,075 --> 00:08:58,585
mast, and some are controlled by remote

247
00:08:58,585 --> 00:08:59,615
from the ground.

248
00:08:59,966 --> 00:09:01,389
Even from the cab,

249
00:09:01,389 --> 00:09:04,472
the operator often cannot see the load,

250
00:09:04,472 --> 00:09:06,924
for example when it is behind a

251
00:09:06,924 --> 00:09:09,137
wall or several floors down.

252
00:09:09,317 --> 00:09:11,574
So a signaler on the ground or

253
00:09:11,574 --> 00:09:14,207
on the building guides each lift by

254
00:09:14,207 --> 00:09:14,658
radio.

255
00:09:14,838 --> 00:09:17,302
The operator moves the load blind,

256
00:09:17,302 --> 00:09:19,621
trusting the voice in their ear.

257
00:09:19,972 --> 00:09:22,183
Why do some tower cranes have a

258
00:09:22,183 --> 00:09:24,323
jib that tilts up into the sky

259
00:09:24,323 --> 00:09:26,392
instead of sticking out flat?

260
00:09:26,572 --> 00:09:28,776
Because in a crowded city,

261
00:09:28,776 --> 00:09:32,083
a flat jib would swing over neighboring

262
00:09:32,083 --> 00:09:34,966
buildings, roads and other cranes.

263
00:09:35,146 --> 00:09:38,336
A luffing jib crane changes its reach

264
00:09:38,336 --> 00:09:41,009
by raising or lowering the jib.

265
00:09:41,189 --> 00:09:43,171
When it does not need to reach

266
00:09:43,171 --> 00:09:45,748
far, the jib points almost straight up,

267
00:09:45,748 --> 00:09:47,400
taking very little space.

268
00:09:47,751 --> 00:09:50,803
Swinging a load over someone else's property

269
00:09:50,803 --> 00:09:52,329
is called oversailing,

270
00:09:52,329 --> 00:09:54,965
and in many cities it needs permission

271
00:09:54,965 --> 00:09:56,005
from the owner.

272
00:09:56,185 --> 00:09:58,588
Luffing cranes avoid most of it.

273
00:09:58,768 --> 00:10:01,760
They also let many cranes work close

274
00:10:01,760 --> 00:10:03,504
together on one site,

275
00:10:03,504 --> 00:10:06,163
each one raising its jib to pass

276
00:10:06,163 --> 00:10:08,323
above or below the others.

277
00:10:08,674 --> 00:10:10,566
The price is complexity.

278
00:10:10,746 --> 00:10:13,230
A luffing crane needs a strong winch

279
00:10:13,230 --> 00:10:15,369
to raise and lower the jib with

280
00:10:15,369 --> 00:10:17,508
the load, and the jib itself is

281
00:10:17,508 --> 00:10:18,060
heavier.

282
00:10:18,240 --> 00:10:20,430
But in places like London,

283
00:10:20,430 --> 00:10:22,284
New York or Hong Kong,

284
00:10:22,284 --> 00:10:25,232
where every meter of sky is shared,

285
00:10:25,232 --> 00:10:28,517
the forest of upward pointing cranes is

286
00:10:28,517 --> 00:10:29,781
a common sight.

287
00:10:30,131 --> 00:10:32,507
Why can this crane arrive on a

288
00:10:32,507 --> 00:10:34,962
truck and stand up by itself in

289
00:10:34,962 --> 00:10:35,912
a few hours?

290
00:10:36,092 --> 00:10:39,064
Because it is a self erecting crane.

291
00:10:39,244 --> 00:10:41,728
The mast and jib are folded for

292
00:10:41,728 --> 00:10:44,932
transport, and the crane carries its own

293
00:10:44,932 --> 00:10:46,454
motors and ballast.

294
00:10:46,634 --> 00:10:48,651
On site, it unfolds itself,

295
00:10:48,651 --> 00:10:51,415
raising the mast and swinging out the

296
00:10:51,415 --> 00:10:54,030
jib, without another crane to help.

297
00:10:54,379 --> 00:10:57,403
These cranes are made for smaller jobs,

298
00:10:57,403 --> 00:11:00,350
houses, low buildings and renovations,

299
00:11:00,350 --> 00:11:03,219
where bringing a big mobile crane for

300
00:11:03,219 --> 00:11:05,235
weeks would cost too much.

301
00:11:05,415 --> 00:11:07,493
They lift less and reach less,

302
00:11:07,493 --> 00:11:09,363
but they are fast to set up

303
00:11:09,363 --> 00:11:11,303
and easy to move to the next

304
00:11:11,303 --> 00:11:11,649
site.

305
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:14,555
Because they stand on a small base

306
00:11:14,555 --> 00:11:17,713
with ballast instead of a deep foundation,

307
00:11:17,713 --> 00:11:20,344
they rely on the ground being level

308
00:11:20,344 --> 00:11:21,020
and firm.

309
00:11:21,200 --> 00:11:22,464
A soft corner,

310
00:11:22,464 --> 00:11:24,902
or a missing outrigger pad,

311
00:11:24,902 --> 00:11:27,249
is enough to tip one over.

312
00:11:27,429 --> 00:11:30,396
Even a small crane follows the same

313
00:11:30,396 --> 00:11:31,922
rule as a big one.

314
00:11:32,102 --> 00:11:33,797
The ground decides.

315
00:11:34,147 --> 00:11:36,911
Why do so many tower crane accidents

316
00:11:36,911 --> 00:11:38,907
happen not during lifting,

317
00:11:38,907 --> 00:11:41,364
but while the crane is being put

318
00:11:41,364 --> 00:11:42,669
up or taken down?

319
00:11:42,849 --> 00:11:44,958
Because in those moments,

320
00:11:44,958 --> 00:11:46,982
the crane is incomplete.

321
00:11:47,162 --> 00:11:48,663
Parts are missing,

322
00:11:48,663 --> 00:11:50,665
the balance is changing,

323
00:11:50,665 --> 00:11:53,417
and pins or bolts are removed one

324
00:11:53,417 --> 00:11:54,001
by one.

325
00:11:54,181 --> 00:11:56,191
A step done out of order can

326
00:11:56,191 --> 00:11:58,488
leave a section held by nothing.

327
00:11:58,838 --> 00:12:01,437
In April two thousand nineteen,

328
00:12:01,437 --> 00:12:04,540
in Seattle, a tower crane being taken

329
00:12:04,540 --> 00:12:07,727
apart fell onto a street during strong

330
00:12:07,727 --> 00:12:08,230
winds.

331
00:12:08,410 --> 00:12:10,204
Four people were killed,

332
00:12:10,204 --> 00:12:12,148
two of them in cars below.

333
00:12:12,328 --> 00:12:16,054
Investigators found that pins holding mast sections

334
00:12:16,054 --> 00:12:18,391
together had been removed early,

335
00:12:18,391 --> 00:12:21,021
before the sections were ready to be

336
00:12:21,021 --> 00:12:22,847
lifted off, to save time.

337
00:12:23,197 --> 00:12:26,641
That is why makers publish exact sequences

338
00:12:26,641 --> 00:12:29,019
for erection and dismantling,

339
00:12:29,019 --> 00:12:30,413
with wind limits,

340
00:12:30,413 --> 00:12:33,693
and why many places now require trained,

341
00:12:33,693 --> 00:12:36,153
certified crews for this work.

342
00:12:36,333 --> 00:12:39,038
The crane is strongest when complete.

343
00:12:39,218 --> 00:12:41,374
The moments in between are where it

344
00:12:41,374 --> 00:12:42,051
is weakest.

345
00:12:42,401 --> 00:12:44,482
How does the last crane get down

346
00:12:44,482 --> 00:12:46,953
from the top of a finished skyscraper?

347
00:12:47,133 --> 00:12:48,723
It needs another crane.

348
00:12:48,903 --> 00:12:51,031
When the building is done,

349
00:12:51,031 --> 00:12:53,322
a smaller crane is set up on

350
00:12:53,322 --> 00:12:54,058
the roof.

351
00:12:54,238 --> 00:12:56,489
It takes the big crane apart and

352
00:12:56,489 --> 00:12:57,756
lowers the pieces.

353
00:12:57,936 --> 00:13:00,834
Then an even smaller crane takes that

354
00:13:00,834 --> 00:13:02,479
one apart, and so on,

355
00:13:02,479 --> 00:13:05,142
until the last one is small enough

356
00:13:05,142 --> 00:13:08,118
to carry down in the building's lifts.

357
00:13:08,467 --> 00:13:11,252
On the tallest towers in the world,

358
00:13:11,252 --> 00:13:13,320
this chain can take weeks.

359
00:13:13,500 --> 00:13:16,857
Every piece must be lowered carefully past

360
00:13:16,857 --> 00:13:19,654
windows that are already installed.

361
00:13:19,834 --> 00:13:22,472
The holes in floors and walls where

362
00:13:22,472 --> 00:13:25,110
the crane stood are then closed up,

363
00:13:25,110 --> 00:13:26,617
and from the street,

364
00:13:26,617 --> 00:13:29,029
nobody can tell a crane was ever

365
00:13:29,029 --> 00:13:29,482
there.

366
00:13:29,831 --> 00:13:31,471
It is the last trick of a

367
00:13:31,471 --> 00:13:33,767
machine that is built to disappear.

368
00:13:33,947 --> 00:13:35,488
It arrives in pieces,

369
00:13:35,488 --> 00:13:37,250
grows with the building,

370
00:13:37,250 --> 00:13:38,131
holds it up,

371
00:13:38,131 --> 00:13:40,112
and leaves without a trace.

372
00:13:40,292 --> 00:13:42,309
All while staying upright,

373
00:13:42,309 --> 00:13:44,325
thanks to a counterweight,

374
00:13:44,325 --> 00:13:46,730
a foundation and a set of rules

375
00:13:46,730 --> 00:13:49,522
written in other people's accidents.

376
00:13:49,872 --> 00:13:51,823
So here are the eleven.

377
00:13:52,003 --> 00:13:54,767
Concrete blocks on the short arm,

378
00:13:54,767 --> 00:13:57,196
and a hook that lifts less at

379
00:13:57,196 --> 00:13:57,866
the tip.

380
00:13:58,046 --> 00:13:59,771
A hidden foundation,

381
00:13:59,771 --> 00:14:01,668
and a mast that grows.

382
00:14:01,848 --> 00:14:03,942
A crane set free to spin in

383
00:14:03,942 --> 00:14:06,812
the wind, turning on a giant bearing,

384
00:14:06,812 --> 00:14:09,604
with an operator sitting at the top.

385
00:14:09,784 --> 00:14:11,706
An arm that points up,

386
00:14:11,706 --> 00:14:14,152
a crane that unfolds itself,

387
00:14:14,152 --> 00:14:16,685
pins that came out too early,

388
00:14:16,685 --> 00:14:19,306
and a crane that needs a crane

389
00:14:19,306 --> 00:14:20,442
to come down.

390
00:14:20,791 --> 00:14:22,900
Next time you see a tower crane

391
00:14:22,900 --> 00:14:25,348
turning slowly over a city at night,

392
00:14:25,348 --> 00:14:27,593
you will know it is doing exactly

393
00:14:27,593 --> 00:14:29,429
what it was designed to do.

394
00:14:29,609 --> 00:14:32,545
If you want more machines explained part

395
00:14:32,545 --> 00:14:35,333
by part, subscribe to Machine Reverse.

396
00:14:35,513 --> 00:14:37,558
And tell us in the comments which

397
00:14:37,558 --> 00:14:39,603
machine you want us to take apart

398
00:14:39,603 --> 00:14:39,913
next.
